<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federico Ponce Vaides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federico Ponce Vaides (1889–1956) was a Guatemalan general who became provisional president in July 1944 after protests forced the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a> to resign. When he tried to keep power through a managed election, a revolt by young officers, students and workers overthrew him on 20 October 1944, the start of the ten-year Guatemalan Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federico-ponce-vaides.jpg" alt="Federico Ponce Vaides" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobierno de Guatemala, Corte Suprema de Justicia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federicoponcevaides_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Federico Ponce Vaides was born on 27 March 1889 into a wealthy upper-class family in Cobán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alta-verapaz">Alta Verapaz</a>, the son of Mariano Ponce Contreras and Victoria Vaides Arrivillaga. Under the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a> he took part in the National Campaign of 1906 against Honduras and El Salvador, and he afterwards served as commander and political leader in several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. After Cabrera's fall in 1920 his brother was executed during a popular uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a>, dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> since 1931, was forced to resign on 1 July 1944 by a popular pro-democracy movement and named three generals, Ponce Vaides, Eduardo Villagran Ariza and Buenaventura Pineda, to a junta to succeed him. On 3 July Ponce Vaides forced the Guatemalan congress at gunpoint to appoint him interim president. He pledged free elections while suppressing the protests: freedom of the press was suspended, arbitrary detentions continued and memorial services for slain revolutionaries were prohibited.</p><p>Protest spread to the countryside, the police were used to intimidate the indigenous population ahead of the election, and progressive officers began to plot a coup. After Alejandro Cordova, editor of the opposition newspaper El Imparcial, was assassinated on 1 October 1944, the plotters reached out to the protest leaders. Ponce Vaides tried to exploit Ladino fears, paying thousands of Indigenous peasants to march in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in his support and promising them land if they backed Ubico's Liberal party.</p><p>On 19 October a small group of officers led by Francisco Javier Arana and Jacobo Árbenz Guzmán launched a coup from within the army, joined the next day by other army factions and civilians. The fighting went against them at first, but unionists and students swelled their ranks, the police and loyal troops were subdued, and Ponce Vaides surrendered unconditionally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ponce Vaides and Ubico were allowed to leave the country safely. A new three-man junta of Árbenz, Arana and Jorge Toriello promised free elections to the presidency, the congress and a constituent assembly. The presidential election was won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo">Juan José Arévalo</a>, whose social and economic reforms began the Guatemalan Revolution. Ponce Vaides died on 29 January 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Federico Ponce Vaides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1932" aria-label="Guatemala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2075 1619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Federico Ponce Vaides"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2525 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2525 1889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
